<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Narayanapala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th–10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Narayanapala was a king of the Pala dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> who reigned for over fifty years in the second half of the ninth century. He belonged to a collateral line that came to the throne after Devapala, and under him the Palas lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> for a time to the Gurjara-Pratiharas. A Buddhist, he also endowed temples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>, and he is named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badal-pillar-inscription">Badal pillar inscription</a>.</p></div>
